In online RL, the agent uses the policy’s uncertainty to balance between trying new actions (exploration) to discover better rewards versus using known good actions (exploitation) for immediate gains ( Lattimore and Szepesvári, 2020 ) . Unfortunately, most policies are either deterministic or parametrized as a distribution with learned variance, only provide aleatoric (data) uncertainty ( Chua et al., 2018 ; Bui et al., 2025 ) , and are often overconfident under distribution shifts. Without epistemic (model) uncertainty, such overconfident models usually get stuck in suboptimal policies, thereby hindering exploration of the optimal policy in the online phase ( Osband et al., 2023a ; Osband et al., 2023b ; Osband et al., 2016 ) . Therefore, to obtain the epistemic uncertainty of π θ ​ ( a | s ) \pi_{	heta}(a|s) , from the Bayesian perspective, we need to seek the posterior distribution p ⁡ ( θ | 𝒟 ) p(	heta|\mathcal{D}) of the weight θ 	heta from the replay buffer 𝒟 \mathcal{D} ( Kendall and Gal, 2017 ; Gal and Ghahramani, 2016 ; Bellemare et al., 2017 ) . From Thm 3.1 , since π θ ​ ( a | s ) \pi_{	heta}(a|s) is extracted from the distribution of diffusion trajectory p θ ​ ( τ ) p_{	heta}(	au) , an approach may utilize existing diffusion UQ methods that employ the Laplace approximation with the ELBO loss in Eq. 3 (e.g., BayesDiff ( Kou et al., 2024 ) or DiffUQ ( Jazbec et al., 2025 ) ) to the last layer of the denoising network ϵ θ \epsilon_{	heta} . Unfortunately, this is not a principled epistemic UQ by: Remark 3.3 . The Laplace approximation with the ELBO loss in Eq. 3 , i.e. p ⁡ ( θ | 𝒟 ) ≈ 𝒩 ⁡ ( θ ^ , Σ ) p(	heta|\mathcal{D})\approx\mathcal{N}(\hat{	heta},\Sigma) , where θ ^ = arg ⁡ min θ ​ ℒ VLB ​ ( θ ) \hat{	heta}=\arg\min_{	heta}\mathcal{L}_{	ext{VLB}}(	heta) and Σ = ( ∇ θ 2 ​ ℒ VLB ​ ( θ , n ) | θ = θ ^ ) − 1 \Sigma=\left(
